cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  GPAI Compliance Cross-Walk: testing EU AI Act obligations against a public
  disclosure record
message: >-
  If you use this method, the cross-walk data, or the model instrument, please cite
  it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Bradley
    family-names: Quinlan
    affiliation: University of Essex
repository-code: "https://github.com/REPLACE-WITH-USERNAME/gpai-compliance-crosswalk"
abstract: >-
  A replicable protocol for testing a frontier model provider's obligations under
  Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 against a public disclosure record, with a worked example
  drawn from two 2026 agentic containment failures and a model Article 91 request for
  information. The method's claim is deliberately narrow: it establishes whether a
  discloser's own account, on its own terms, resolves a given obligation, and does not
  purport to establish what occurred. That limit is enforced mechanically by an included
  validator. The principal substantive finding is one of scope: Article 3, point (63)
  excludes from the definition of 'general-purpose AI model' those models used for
  research or development before market placement, and Article 2(8) excludes pre-market
  research activity, so the obligations may not attach to the model that caused the
  incident at all. Article 55(1), point (b), which expressly reaches systemic risk
  stemming from development, is what survives.
keywords:
  - EU AI Act
  - general-purpose AI models
  - systemic risk
  - incident reporting
  - AI governance
  - regulatory compliance
  - AI incident response
